1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,229
More than two thousand
years ago,

2
00:00:02,329 --> 00:00:03,848
someone in ancient Greece

3
00:00:03,888 --> 00:00:06,377
built a machine to
predict the sky.

4
00:00:06,827 --> 00:00:08,796
Made of bronze and
packed with gears,

5
00:00:08,846 --> 00:00:11,595
it tracked the cycles
of the Sun and Moon—

6
00:00:11,695 --> 00:00:13,664
and predicted eclipses.

7
00:00:14,114 --> 00:00:17,382
Found in a shipwreck
near Antikythera in 1901,

8
00:00:17,432 --> 00:00:21,221
it became known as the
Antikythera Mechanism.

9
00:00:21,671 --> 00:00:23,380
Today, 82 fragments survive,

10
00:00:23,430 --> 00:00:25,659
including 30 corroded
bronze gears.

11
00:00:25,909 --> 00:00:29,957
Together, they reveal
extraordinary engineering.

12
00:00:30,507 --> 00:00:33,256
But its full design
is still debated.

13
00:00:33,506 --> 00:00:34,695
Some parts are lost,

14
00:00:34,745 --> 00:00:37,564
and researchers are still
piecing together

15
00:00:37,604 --> 00:00:38,533
how it worked.

16
00:00:39,083 --> 00:00:41,122
Often called the
world’s oldest known

17
00:00:41,162 --> 00:00:43,132
analogue computer.

18
00:00:43,582 --> 00:00:47,000
A small machine built
to model an immense sky.
